gideon gora has lived in lima district  all his life age 74 he says over the  years the rains have become more and  more erratic drought induced by the El  Nino weather pattern has cut some always  maize harvest by half and reduced water  levels in dams and rivers we don't eat  in the morning and in the afternoon we  eat only at night we eat as little as  possible so that the food can last  longer president amos minagawa has  declared this latest drought a national  disaster and the United Nations wants  more help to prevent a humanitarian  crisis it's raised its appeal to  international donors to 331 million  dollars truly are marching toward  starvation if we're not there to help  them 2.3 million people in this country  by this fall it will be substantially  higher at 3.6 million people because a  lean season season is coming upon us  then by early next year we're looking at  five point five five point three give or  take those numbers mean about a third of  the country's 15 million population will  need food aid some of the money raised  would be used to buy food provided clean  water and give cash handouts to  vulnerable families but raising all the  money needed could be difficult Zimbabwe  is competing with other crises around  the world such as Syria Yemen and South  Sudan making an already dire situation  even worse tropical cyclone edye  devastated parts of an aquiline province  in the east of the country earlier this  year  heavy rains flash floods and strong  winds destroyed crops leaving thousands  homeless and hungry the cash-strapped  government said aspiring grain from  other countries to replenish stocks we  as government are doing our part we are  doing allows not merely sitting back and  working for our colleagues from abroad  UN and others to assistance were doing  something ourselves and we therefore  ministry looking after social protection  problems but many Zimbabweans already  frustrated by rising inflation and a  weak economy feel their government isn't  doing enough gideon gora is one of them  he only has a few bags of maize left  before he completely runs out  he says he's praying for rain or  humanitarian aid to reach him whichever  comes first  Jairo Matassa al Jazeera Lima Zimbabwe
 

